inute no  relationship to objective reality it  came from the Babylonians and like I  mentioned the Babylonian number system  in general was based on 60 and it's also  how they kept track of time hours  minutes seconds these are all man-made  concepts just like calendars and generic  numeral systems so can we assign meaning  to these numbers and expect it to  correspond with anything that's  happening in the real world in the end  it might be useless to try to explain  this logic to a numerologist because  most numerological claims are mystic or  supernatural and logic doesn't really  apply in that domain numerology is just  another belief system and methodology  whose validity is totally unfalsifiable  it relies on superstition and  attributing meaning to coincidences  numbers are one of the most important  tools of abstraction and abstract  thinking that humans have which gives  numerology a facade of being reasonable  or intellectual math is pure and it's  elegant and it's powerful which is  probably why numerologists are so  tempted to do what they do but to  subscribe to numerology is to entirely  misunderstand what numbers are  metaphysically numbers are just ideas if  we're looking at a room with four chairs  in it the number four doesn't exist in  the room four is a way of thinking about  the chairs numbers can't exist in the  real world much less are they able to  have causal relationships with events in  the real world and that's fundamentally  why numerology fails to add up if you  want to delve deeper into the  philosophical foundations of math check  out a free course from Stanford  University called introduction to  mathematical thinking you can search for  it at class  - central.com forward slash pakman class  central lets you take entire college  courses online from top you  universities whether you're interested  in science politics philosophy  psychology there are tens of thousands  of courses to choose from so you're  bound to find something you're into  check it out at class  - central.com forward slash Pakman


